  "reference": "Numerics @ 2a0357a. Runs corehydro::numerics::support::run_optimizer (core/include/corehydro/numerics/support/optimizer_runner.hpp) against the built-in objectives named by `construct.objective`, mapping onto the same TestFunctions.cs formulas the upstream MSTest classes call (FXYZ/DeJong/Booth/McCormick/FX -- see core/tests/optimization_test_functions.hpp). `parameter` assertions read `OptimResult.parameters[args[0]]`; `value` reads `OptimResult.value` (the raw objective at the optimum, in its own sign convention -- see optimizer_runner.hpp's fill_optimizer_result). Every case's tolerance matches the upstream MSTest `Assert.AreEqual(actual, expected, delta)` literal exactly. Every expected value is the upstream test file's own literal, transcribed verbatim -- never a value computed by this repo's own C++. The optional `construct.gradient` key names an analytic gradient in the same four catalogs (`Grad_FXYZ`/`Grad_DeJong`/`Grad_Booth`), the second host-language callback the `\"adam\"` and `\"gradient_descent\"` methods take; omitted, both classes differentiate numerically exactly as a null C# `Gradient` delegate makes them. The three gradient cases additionally pin `iterations` and `function_evaluations` at exact equality: they are the only quantity that distinguishes a run driven by the supplied gradient from one that silently ignored it (1518 evaluations against 12137 for adam_fxyz), and all three counts were measured identical in the real C# library, in the C++ core compiled with and without floating-point contraction, in R and in Python.",
